  "body": {
    "id": "lem:nonprivate-oracle-bump-lower",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "There is a constant \\(c>0\\), depending only on the fixed class parameters, such that \\(R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}}\\ge c n^{-\\gamma/(2\\gamma+d)}\\) for every \\(n\\).  This is only the direct \\(\\gamma\\)-smooth bump lower bound and makes no assertion about the queued sparse-component nonprivate converse.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "R_NP",
      "n",
      "gamma",
      "d",
      "M_H"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:iid",
      "ass:interior-point",
      "def:holder-class",
      "def:nonprivate-risk"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Use the two direct bump laws from the proof of the private lower envelope, with \\(h=c_h\\Delta^{1/\\gamma}\\) and target separation \\(2\\Delta\\).  They belong to \\(\\mathcal M_H\\) for small fixed constants.  Their one-record laws differ only in the treated binary-outcome cell on a set of volume \\(O(h^d)\\); all cell probabilities are bounded away from zero.  Direct expansion of the finite-cell chi-square divergence therefore gives\n\\[\n \\chi^2(P_+,P_-)\\le C\\Delta^2h^d.\n\\]\nIndependence gives\n\\[\n 1+\\chi^2(P_+^{\\otimes n},P_-^{\\otimes n})\n =\\{1+\\chi^2(P_+,P_-)\\}^n\n \\le\\exp(Cn\\Delta^2h^d).\n\\]\nCauchy--Schwarz applied to the likelihood ratio yields \\(\\operatorname{TV}\\le\\tfrac12\\sqrt{\\chi^2}\\).  Taking \\(\\Delta=c n^{-\\gamma/(2\\gamma+d)}\\) makes \\(n\\Delta^2h^d\\le Cc^{2+d/\\gamma}\\), so a small fixed \\(c\\) makes product total variation at most \\(1/2\\).  The nearest-target testing argument then gives maximum absolute risk at least \\(c\\Delta\\), and infimizing over all nonprivate estimators proves the claim.  No nuisance-varying nonprivate construction is used.",
